Third-party components
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The full attribution manifest is generated at build time by the Nuke `Pack`
target and published to docs/manual/third-party.md. See
docs/plan/01-technology-decisions.md § ADR-015 for the dependency licence audit.

Components currently in use:

  ANTLR 4 (Antlr4.Runtime, Antlr4.CodeGenerator)
    BSD-3-Clause — https://github.com/antlr/antlr4

  Microsoft.CodeAnalysis (Roslyn)
    MIT — https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn

  xunit
    Apache-2.0 — https://github.com/xunit/xunit

  System.IO.Hashing
    MIT — https://github.com/dotnet/runtime

  ZstdSharp.Port
    MIT — https://github.com/oleg-st/ZstdSharp

  K4os.Compression.LZ4
    MIT — https://github.com/MiloszKrajewski/K4os.Compression.LZ4
    Recorded explicitly per ADR-015: the package declares its licence in a file
    rather than as an SPDX expression, so it is not picked up automatically.

  Silk.NET (Silk.NET.SDL, Silk.NET.Core, Silk.NET.Maths)
    MIT — https://github.com/dotnet/Silk.NET

  SDL 2
    Zlib — https://github.com/libsdl-org/SDL
    Bindings only are redistributed here; the library itself is not vendored and
    comes from the system. Recorded because a build that bundles libSDL2 carries
    the obligation, and the place to notice that is before the bundling happens.

  Open Sans
    OFL-1.1 — https://github.com/googlefonts/opensans
    Vendored, and it is the one binary asset the tree carries: the editor embeds
    OpenSans-Regular.ttf and OpenSans-SemiBold.ttf from
    Editor/Vixen.Editor.App/Fonts/, where OFL.txt is the licence as it ships. An
    interface whose text is laid out by whatever face the operating system
    happened to have lays out differently on every machine it runs on, which is
    why this is not left to a system lookup — see `Vixen.Editor.App.Fonts`.

  wgpu-native
    MIT OR Apache-2.0 — https://github.com/gfx-rs/wgpu-native
    Not vendored: `nuke RestoreNativeDeps` fetches the pinned, checksummed release
    named in build/native-dependencies.json into artifacts/, which git ignores.
    Recorded here rather than generated from the archive because these releases
    ship the headers and the libraries and no licence text — so unlike MoltenVK,
    there is nothing to copy out. An application that ships the library carries
    the obligation and has to include the upstream text itself.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Conformance corpora — incorporated as data
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Distinct from both of the categories above, and from the reference material
below. These are third-party test fixtures that are translated or transcribed and
then COMMITTED into this repository, because CI has no reference clone to
generate them from. No implementation code is taken; the expected numbers are,
and those are the copyrightable part. Recorded here per ADR-015, which counts
this as its own case rather than as a stricter kind of reading.

  Yoga — conformance suite
    MIT — https://github.com/facebook/yoga
    534 fixtures translated from tests/generated/*.cpp by Tools/Vixen.YogaTestGen
    into Core/Vixen.Ui.Layout.Tests/Generated/. Yoga also appears under reference
    material below, for the separate matter of its algorithm (ADR-006).

  Taffy — conformance suite
    MIT — https://github.com/DioxusLabs/taffy
    5 524 fixtures vetted and consolidated verbatim from tests/xml/ by
    Tools/Vixen.TaffyTestGen into Core/Vixen.Ui.Layout.Tests/Taffy/Corpus/. Taffy
    is MIT only — unlike most of the Rust ecosystem it is NOT dual MIT/Apache-2.0,
    so it carries no express patent grant and the compatibility argument rests on
    MIT alone. The fixtures are what is incorporated, and Taffy generates them from
    HTML laid out by Chrome-for-Testing.

    This entry used to end "no Taffy source is read, ported or linked". The middle
    two still hold and the first no longer does: src/compute/block.rs was READ
    while writing Core/Vixen.Ui.Layout/LayoutTree.Block.cs, which is why Taffy now
    also appears under reference material below. That is ADR-006's posture for
    Yoga applied a second time — the algorithm is re-derived against CSS 2.1 §8.3.1
    and §10.3.3 and expressed over this store's arrays, with different plumbing
    (the collapsibility input is derived from the tree rather than threaded as a
    layout parameter, there is no BlockContext, and the sizing goes through Yoga's
    SizingMode rather than Taffy's LayoutInput). No file here is a translation of
    a Taffy file and no Taffy code is copied, so the corpus entry above is
    unchanged and nothing needs a §4b modification notice.

  Unicode Character Database — UAX #9, #14, #29 conformance data
    Unicode-3.0 — https://www.unicode.org/Public/UCD/latest/

  text-rendering-tests
    Apache-2.0, fonts under OFL-1.1 — https://github.com/unicode-org/text-rendering-tests
    The one case where the test *fonts* are redistributed too; see
    Core/Vixen.Ui.Text.Tests/Fonts/README.md.

